PART 8: COMMON MISTAKES TO AVOID

================================================================================

Mistake 1: Forgetting the Key Prop

// ❌ WRONG - No key prop
{users.map(user => (
  <UserProfileCard user={user} />
))}

// ✓ CORRECT - Has key prop
{users.map(user => (
  <UserProfileCard key={user.id} user={user} />
))}
Why it matters:

• React uses the key to identify which card is which • Without it, React can get confused when the list changes • This can cause: • State to get mixed up between cards • Animations to behave unexpectedly • Performance issues • Bugs that are hard to debug • Always use a unique, stable identifier like
id

================================================================================

Mistake 2: Mutating State Directly

// ❌ WRONG - Mutates the original array
const deleteUser = (id) => {
  users.splice(id, 1)
  setUsers(users)
}

// ✓ CORRECT - Creates a new array
const deleteUser = (id) => {
  setUsers(users.filter(user => user.id !== id))
}
Why it matters:

• React detects changes by comparing the old and new state • If you mutate the original array, React might not detect the change • This can cause: • UI not updating when it should • Unexpected behavior • Hard-to-debug bugs • Always create a new array/object when updating state

================================================================================

Mistake 3: Not Destructuring Props

// ❌ Less clean
function UserProfileCard(props) {
  return <h2>{props.user.name}</h2>
}

// ✓ Cleaner and more professional
function UserProfileCard({ user }) {
  return <h2>{user.name}</h2>
}
Why it matters:

• Destructuring makes code: • Cleaner and easier to read • Clearer about what props are needed • More professional and industry-standard • Easier to maintain

================================================================================

Mistake 4: Using Index as Key

// ❌ WRONG - Using index as key
{users.map((user, index) => (
  <UserProfileCard key={index} user={user} />
))}

// ✓ CORRECT - Using unique id
{users.map(user => (
  <UserProfileCard key={user.id} user={user} />
))}
Why it matters:

• If the list order changes or items are added/removed, the index changes • This causes: • React to lose track of which card is which • State to get mixed up • Animations to break • Bugs that are hard to find • Always use a stable, unique identifier like
id

